<section class="col" id="avis">
  <span class="num">07</span>
  <div class="avis-head"><h2>My take on it</h2>
    <span class="human" role="note" aria-label="Written by a human without AI, translated with AI"><Fragment set:html={pencil} /><span><b>100% human</b><small>written without AI · AI-translated</small></span></span></div>
  <div class="hm">
  <p>On the parameter <b>β</b>, the exhaustion of ideas: it can indeed be estimated “after the fact”, but the question of predicting it is utterly fascinating. <b>β</b> is a measure of the space of ideas being explored. We don't even know whether, in the case of AI, this space is finite or not. We know, for example, that the space of ideas of Euclidean geometry (those of Pythagoras and Thales) is infinite, but “decidable”: a machine can settle any question you ask it on the subject. But for AI research, it is the great unknown. Even more than its size (finite or infinite), what matters is its structure: are there crucial ideas still undiscovered, but enormously hard to reach?</p>
  <p>On the parameter <b>λ</b>, I think the big AI labs have a fairly precise idea: they must have run experiments on solving R&amp;D tasks while varying the number of “artificial researcher” agents and measuring the time to solution. My intuition is that <b>λ</b> is probably better for a team of AI agents than for a team of human researchers (I'm sure you see what I mean ;)). Among the clues, OpenAI for example deployed 10,000 “artificial researcher” agents on the Navier-Stokes millennium problem (another singularity story). Why 10,000? Did they test various social organizations, or let them organize themselves? OpenAI and Anthropic have the results tables, we don't. And they will probably never be shared with us. It seems fairly straightforward to get first estimates of <b>λ</b>, but you need the resources to set up the experimental protocols: lots of tokens, and therefore lots of money.</p>
  <p>My intuition (nothing scientific about it): <span class="m">r</span> &gt; 1. <b>λ</b> seems high to me: the agents probably coordinate very efficiently. As for <b>β</b>, I think it is low: ideas still seem “easy” to find. AIs are cutting through our supposedly near-impossible problems like a knife through butter: OpenAI knocked out hundreds of major math problems in a few weeks. For my own, more personal assessment, I use a scientific problem from my PhD thesis as a personal benchmark: I started working on it with Opus 4.6. With each new version, I make progress on the theory, and the performance of the algorithms produced keeps improving. I saw the biggest jumps with the move to Fable 5, then Fable 5.2.</p>
  <p>But this “natural” return is going to face headwinds: increased caution among the engineers at the heart of the reactor, the roughness of reality (curves never quite play out), and, I hope, serious, frank, massive and coordinated regulation of the sector.</p>
  </div>
</section>

